      "id": "oligopoly_game_theory",
      "chapter": 18,
      "name": "Oligopoly and Game Theory",
      "definition": "A few firms, interdependent decisions. Each firm's profit depends on what others do.",
      "key_insight": "The prisoners dilemma explains why cartels break down: each firm has an individual incentive to cheat (produce more), even though all firms would be better off cooperating. The Nash equilibrium is where each player is doing the best they can GIVEN what the other is doing.",
      "depends_on": [
        "profit_maximization"
      ],
      "leads_to": [],
      "equations": [
        {
          "name": "nash_equilibrium",
          "formula": "Each player's strategy is the best response to the other's strategy",
          "when": "Payoff matrix questions -- find where neither player wants to change"
        },
        {
          "name": "dominant_strategy",
          "formula": "A strategy that is best regardless of what the opponent does",
          "when": "Check each player's best response in every scenario"
        }
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "A Nash equilibrium is NOT necessarily the best outcome for both players. In the prisoners dilemma, both defecting is Nash but both cooperating is better.",
        "When a cartel breaks down, output RISES and price FALLS."
      ],
      "butler_frequency": "high",
