      "image": "slide_graphs/WEEK_10_GROWTH_SAVINGS_AND_INVESTMENT_s007.png",
      "vision_extract": "## SLIDE 7 ANALYSIS: Correcting Variables for Inflation\n\n### 1) Main Concept/Topic\n**Inflation Adjustment of Economic Variables** - Converting nominal dollar amounts from different time periods into real terms using price level adjustments to enable meaningful comparisons across time.\n\n### 2) Equations Shown\n**Primary Formula:**\n```\nAmount in today's dollars = Amount in year T dollars \u00d7 (Price level today)/(Price level in year T)\n```\n\n**Applied Calculation:**\n```\n$1.25 \u00d7 234.6/30.9 = $9.49 in 2013 dollars\n```\n\n### 3) Graph Details\nNo graphs present on this slide.\n\n### 4) Key Definitions or Emphasized Text\n\n**Key Definition:**\n- **Real vs. Nominal Adjustment**: Method for converting dollar figures from different time periods to make them comparable by accounting for inflation\n\n**Emphasized Example Data:**\n- **Time Periods**: \"year T\" = 1963, \"today\" = 2013\n- **Original Value**: Minimum wage was $1.25 in 1963\n- **Price Indices**: \n  - CPI in 1963 (year T) = 30.9\n  - CPI in 2013 (today) = 234.6\n- **Result**: $9.49 in 2013 dollars (shown in red text for emphasis)\n\n**Emphasized Text**: The final converted amount \"$9.49 in 2013 dollars\" is highlighted in red to show the inflation-adjusted real value.",
      "text": "Correcting Variables for Inflation\n\u2022 Dollar figures from different times\nAmount in today\u2032s dollars =\nPrice level today\n= Amount in year T dollars \u00d7\nPrice level in year T\n\u2022 In our example:\n\u2013 \u201cyear T \u201d is 1963, \u201ctoday\u201d is 2013\n\u2013 Min wage was $1.25 in year T\n\u2013 CPI = 30.9 in year T, CPI = 234.6 today\n\u2013 "
    },
