      "image": "slide_graphs/WEEK_10_GROWTH_SAVINGS_AND_INVESTMENT_s087.png",
      "vision_extract": "## Slide 87 Analysis: The Slope of the Supply Curve\n\n### 1) Main Concept/Topic\nThe slope of the supply curve for loanable funds and the relationship between interest rates and savings behavior.\n\n### 2) Equations Shown\nNo equations are displayed on this slide.\n\n### 3) Graph Details\n\n**Axes:**\n- X-axis: Loanable Funds ($ billions)\n- Y-axis: Real Interest Rate\n\n**Curves:**\n- Single upward-sloping supply curve labeled \"Supply\"\n\n**Key Points:**\n- Point at (60, 3%): intersection of 60 billion dollars in loanable funds at 3% interest rate\n- Point at (80, 6%): intersection of 80 billion dollars in loanable funds at 6% interest rate\n\n**Labels and Features:**\n- Horizontal dashed lines at 3% and 6% interest rates\n- Vertical dashed line at 80 billion dollars\n- Blue arrow indicating the movement from the lower point to the higher point along the supply curve\n\n### 4) Key Definitions or Emphasized Text\n**Main emphasized concept:** \"An increase in the interest rate makes saving more attractive, which increases the quantity of loanable funds supplied.\"\n\nThis text explains the positive relationship between interest rates and the supply of loanable funds, demonstrating why the supply curve slopes upward.",
      "text": "The slope of the supply curve\nAn increase in the\ninterest rate makes\nReal\nsaving more\nInterest Supply\nattractive, which\nRate\nincreases the\n6%\nquantity of loanable\nfunds supplied.\n3%\n60 80 Loanable Funds\n($ billions)\nMankiw, Principles of Economics, 10th Edition. \u00a9 2024 Cengage. All Rights Reserved. "
    },
