      "vision_extract": "## Slide 89 Analysis\n\n### 1) Main Concept/Topic\nThe slope of the demand curve for loanable funds - demonstrating the inverse relationship between real interest rates and quantity of loanable funds demanded.\n\n### 2) Equations Shown\nNo equations present on this slide.\n\n### 3) Graph Details\n**Axes:**\n- Y-axis: Real Interest Rate (percentage)\n- X-axis: Loanable Funds ($ billions)\n\n**Curves:**\n- Single downward-sloping demand curve labeled \"Demand\"\n\n**Key Points:**\n- Point 1: 7% interest rate, 50 billion dollars\n- Point 2: 4% interest rate, 80 billion dollars\n\n**Labels/Annotations:**\n- Dashed horizontal and vertical lines marking the two key points\n- Red arrow showing movement from 7% to 4% interest rate\n- Blue arrow showing movement from 50 to 80 billion dollars in loanable funds\n\n**Intersections:**\n- Two coordinate points where dashed lines intersect the demand curve at (50, 7%) and (80, 4%)\n\n### 4) Key Definitions/Emphasized Text\n**Main explanatory text:** \"A fall in the real interest rate reduces the cost of borrowing, which increases the quantity of loanable funds demanded.\"\n\n**Title emphasis:** \"The slope of the demand curve\" (in red text)\n\n**Key concept:** The inverse relationship between interest rates and demand for loanable funds is demonstrated through the downward slope of the demand curve.",
      "text": "The slope of the demand curve\nA fall in the real interest\nReal rate reduces the cost of\nInterest borrowing, which\nRate\nincreases the quantity\n7%\nof loanable funds\ndemanded.\n4%\nDemand\n50 80 Loanable Funds\n($ billions)\nMankiw, Principles of Economics, 10th Edition. \u00a9 2024 Cengage. All Rights Reserved."
    },
