      "vision_extract": "## Slide Analysis - Xavier's Total Cost Curve\n\n### 1) Main Concept/Topic\nTotal Cost Curve analysis in Producer Theory - demonstrating the relationship between quantity of output and total production costs\n\n### 2) Equations Shown\nNo explicit equations are displayed on this slide.\n\n### 3) Graph Details\n**Axes:**\n- X-axis: Quantity of Output (Q), ranging from 0 to 100\n- Y-axis: Total Cost, ranging from $200 to $500\n\n**Curve:**\n- Red upward-sloping total cost curve\n- Shows increasing total costs as output increases\n- Curve appears to have increasing slope (convex shape), indicating increasing marginal costs\n\n**Key Points Plotted:**\n- (0, $200)\n- (30, $250)\n- (55, $300)\n- (75, $350)\n- (90, $400)\n- (100, $450)\n\n**Labels:**\n- Vertical dashed lines at Q = 30, 55, 75, 90, and 100\n- Horizontal dashed grid lines at cost intervals\n\n### 4) Key Definitions or Emphasized Text\n**Data Table:**\n- Q buckets: 0, 30, 55, 75, 90, 100\n- Corresponding Total Costs: $200, $250, $300, $350, $400, $450\n- Fixed cost appears to be $200 (cost when Q = 0)\n- Variable costs increase as output increases from 0 to 100 buckets\n\nThe slide demonstrates how total costs rise with production quantity, with the curve showing both fixed costs (y-intercept at $200) and increasing variable costs.",
      "text": "Xavier\u2019s Total Cost Curve\nTotal Cost\n$500\nQ Total 450\nbuckets Cost 400\n350\n0 $200\n300\n30 $250\n250\n200\n55 $300\n75 $350\n90 $400\n100 $450 Q\n0 30 55 75 90 100\nQuantity of Output\nMankiw, Principles of Economics, 10th Edition. \u00a9 2024 Cengage. All Rights Reserved. May not be scanned, copied or duplicated, "
    },
