      "image": "slide_graphs/WEEK_5_PRODUCER_THEORY_s055.png",
      "vision_extract": "## Slide 55 Analysis: Angel's FC, VC, and TC curves\n\n### 1) Main Concept/Topic\nProducer cost curves analysis showing the relationship between Fixed Cost (FC), Variable Cost (VC), and Total Cost (TC) curves using Angel's business example.\n\n### 2) Equations Shown\nNo explicit equations are shown, but the implicit relationship is demonstrated:\n- TC = FC + VC\n\n### 3) Graph Details\n**Axes:**\n- X-axis: Quantity of output (Q) - ranges from 0 to 10\n- Y-axis: Cost - ranges from $0 to $160\n\n**Curves:**\n- **FC curve (blue):** Horizontal line at $18 across all output levels\n- **VC curve (dark blue):** Upward sloping curve starting at origin, showing increasing variable costs\n- **TC curve (green):** Upward sloping curve parallel to VC curve, starting at $18\n\n**Key Points:**\n- FC remains constant at $18 for all output levels (Q = 0 to 10)\n- VC starts at $0 when Q = 0 and increases to $136 when Q = 10\n- TC starts at $18 when Q = 0 and reaches $154 when Q = 10\n\n### 4) Key Definitions or Emphasized Text\n- **\"The TC and VC curves are parallel\"** - highlighting the constant vertical distance between these curves\n- **\"The FC curve is a horizontal line\"** - emphasizing that fixed costs don't change with output level\n\n**Data Table:** Shows numerical values for Q (0-10), FC (constant at 18), VC (0-136), and TC (18-154)",
      "text": "Angel\u2019s FC, VC, and TC curves\nQ FC VC TC Cost\n$160\n0 18 0 18\nTC\n1 18 15 33 120\nVC\n2 18 25 43\n80\n3 18 30 48\n4 18 32 50 40\n5 18 36 54\nFC\n0\n6 18 44 62\n0 2 4 6 8 10 Q\nQuantity of output\n7 18 58 76\n8 18 78 96 The TC and VC curves are parallel\n9 18 104 122 The FC curve is a horizontal line\n10 18 136 154\nM"
    },
