      "image": "slide_graphs/WEEK_5_PRODUCER_THEORY_s058.png",
      "vision_extract": "## Slide 58 Analysis: Angel's AFC Curve\n\n### 1) Main Concept/Topic\nAverage Fixed Cost (AFC) curve behavior and calculation in producer theory\n\n### 2) Equations Shown\nNo explicit equations displayed, but the AFC calculation is implicit: AFC = FC/Q (Fixed Cost divided by Quantity)\n\n### 3) Graph Details\n**Axes:**\n- X-axis: Quantity of output (Q), ranging from 0 to 10\n- Y-axis: Costs, ranging from $0 to $35.0\n\n**Curve:**\n- Single downward-sloping curve labeled \"AFC\"\n- Hyperbolic shape (rectangular hyperbola)\n- Continuously decreasing as quantity increases\n- Approaches zero asymptotically but never touches the x-axis\n\n**Key Points:**\n- Starts at Q=1 with AFC=$18.0\n- At Q=2: AFC=$9.0\n- At Q=5: AFC=$3.6\n- At Q=10: AFC=$1.8\n- Blue dots mark specific data points from the table\n\n### 4) Key Definitions or Emphasized Text\n**Table showing three columns:**\n- **Q**: Quantity (0 through 10)\n- **FC**: Fixed Cost (constant at $18 for all positive quantities)\n- **AFC**: Average Fixed Cost (decreasing from 18.0 to 1.8)\n\n**Key insight**: AFC continuously declines as output increases because fixed costs are spread over larger quantities, demonstrating the concept of spreading fixed costs over production volume.",
      "text": "Angel\u2019s AFC curve\nCosts\nQ FC AFC 35.0\n$\n0 18 -\n30.0\n1 18 18.0\n25.0\n2 18 9.0\n3 18 6.0\n20.0\n4 18 4.5\n15.0\n5 18 3.6\n6 18 3.0 10.0\n7 18 2.6\n5.0 AFC\n8 18 2.3\n0.0\n9 18 2.0\n0 2 4 6 8 10 Q\n10 18 1.8\nQuantity of output\nMankiw, Principles of Economics, 10th Edition. \u00a9 2024 Cengage. All Rights Reserved. May no"
    },
    {
      "id": "72637cc22875",
      "deck": "WEEK_5_PRODUCER_THEORY",
