      "image": "slide_graphs/WEEK_5_PRODUCER_THEORY_s059.png",
      "vision_extract": "## Slide 59 Analysis: Angel's AVC and ATC Curves\n\n### 1) Main Concept/Topic\nAverage Variable Cost (AVC) and Average Total Cost (ATC) curves analysis, demonstrating the relationship between these cost measures and identifying the efficient scale of production.\n\n### 2) Equations Shown\nNo explicit equations are displayed, but the data implies:\n- AVC = VC/Q (Average Variable Cost = Variable Cost \u00f7 Quantity)\n- ATC = TC/Q (Average Total Cost = Total Cost \u00f7 Quantity)\n\n### 3) Graph Details\n**Axes:**\n- X-axis: Quantity of output (Q), ranging from 0 to 10\n- Y-axis: Cost ($), ranging from 0 to approximately 35.0\n\n**Curves:**\n- **AVC curve (blue)**: U-shaped curve starting at Q=1 ($15.0), decreasing to minimum at Q=6 ($7.3), then increasing\n- **ATC curve (green)**: U-shaped curve starting at Q=1 ($33.0), decreasing to minimum at Q=5 ($10.8), then increasing\n\n**Key Points:**\n- AVC minimum: Q=6, Cost=$7.3\n- ATC minimum: Q=5, Cost=$10.8\n- Vertical dashed line at Q=5 indicating efficient scale\n\n**Intersections:**\n- ATC curve lies above AVC curve at all production levels\n- Both curves are U-shaped with ATC reaching its minimum before AVC\n\n### 4) Key Definitions/Emphasized Text\n**\"Efficient scale: quantity that minimizes ATC\"** - This is highlighted in blue text with an arrow pointing to Q=5, where ATC reaches its minimum value of $10.8.\n\nThe data table shows complete cost breakdown for quantities 0-10, with Variable Cost (VC), Total Cost (TC), Average Variable Cost (AVC), and Average Total Cost (ATC) values.",
      "text": "Angel\u2019s AVC and ATC curves\nQ VC TC AVC ATC\nCosts\n35.0\n0 $0 $18 - - $\nEfficient scale:\n30.0\n1 15 33 15.0 33.0 quantity that\n2 25 43 12.5 21.5 25.0 minimizes ATC\n3 30 48 10.0 16.0\n20.0\nATC\n4 32 50 8.0 12.5\n15.0\n5 36 54 7.2 10.8\n10.0\n6 44 62 7.3 10.3\nAVC\n7 58 76 8.3 10.9\n5.0\n8 78 96 9.8 12.0\n0.0\n9 104 "
    },
