      "vision_extract": "## Slide 68 Analysis: LRATC with 3 Factory Sizes\n\n### 1) Main Concept/Topic\nLong-Run Average Total Cost (LRATC) construction with multiple factory sizes and the relationship between short-run and long-run cost curves in producer theory.\n\n### 2) Equations Shown\nNo explicit equations are presented on this slide.\n\n### 3) Graph Details\n**Axes:**\n- X-axis: Q (Quantity)\n- Y-axis: Avg Total Cost\n\n**Curves:**\n- Three U-shaped short-run average total cost curves:\n  - ATC_S (dark blue, leftmost)\n  - ATC_M (green, middle) \n  - ATC_L (purple, rightmost)\n\n**Key Features:**\n- All three ATC curves are U-shaped\n- The curves are positioned sequentially from left to right\n- Each curve represents a different factory size (S, M, L)\n- The curves appear to have different minimum points at different output levels\n\n### 4) Key Definitions or Emphasized Text\n- **Factory size options:** Firm can choose from three factory sizes: **S, M, L**\n- **SRATC curves:** Each size has its own **SRATC curve** (Short-Run Average Total Cost)\n- **Long-run flexibility:** The firm can change to a different factory size in the **long run**, but **not in the short run**\n- **LRATC concept:** This slide demonstrates the building blocks for constructing the Long-Run Average Total Cost curve from multiple short-run curves",
      "text": "LRATC with 3 factory sizes\n\u2022 Firm can choose\nfrom three factory\nAvg\nsizes: S, M, L.\nTotal\nCost Each size has its\nATC ATC\nS ATC\nown SRATC curve.\nM L\n\u2022 The firm can\nchange to a\ndifferent factory\nsize in the long\nrun, but not in the\nQ\nshort run.\nMankiw, Principles of Economics, 10th Edition. \u00a9 2024 Cen"
    },
