      "vision_extract": "## Slide 70 Analysis: A Typical LRATC Curve\n\n### 1) Main Concept/Topic\nLong-Run Average Total Cost (LRATC) curve construction and characteristics in producer theory\n\n### 2) Equations Shown\nNone\n\n### 3) Graph Details\n**Axes:**\n- Vertical axis: ATC (Average Total Cost)\n- Horizontal axis: Q (Quantity)\n\n**Curves:**\n- Multiple black U-shaped curves representing individual SRATC (Short-Run Average Total Cost) curves of different factory sizes\n- One red U-shaped curve labeled \"LRATC\" (Long-Run Average Total Cost)\n\n**Key Points:**\n- The LRATC curve forms the lower envelope of all SRATC curves\n- Each SRATC curve represents a different factory size\n- The LRATC curve is tangent to multiple SRATC curves at various points\n- Overall shape: U-shaped curve that is flatter than individual SRATC curves\n\n**Intersections:**\n- LRATC curve touches (is tangent to) each SRATC curve at exactly one point\n- These tangency points represent the optimal output level for each factory size\n\n### 4) Key Definitions or Emphasized Text\n- **SRATC**: Short-Run Average Total Cost (emphasized in blue text)\n- **LRATC**: Long-Run Average Total Cost (emphasized in red text)\n- **Key concept**: \"In the real world, factories come in many sizes, each with its own SRATC curve\"\n- **Conclusion**: \"So, a typical LRATC curve looks like this\"",
      "text": "A typical LRATC curve\n\u2022 In the real world,\nATC\nfactories come in\nmany sizes, each\nLRATC\nwith its own SRATC\ncurve.\n\u2022 So, a typical LRATC\ncurve looks like this:\nQ\nMankiw, Principles of Economics, 10th Edition. \u00a9 2024 Cengage. All Rights Reserved. May not be scanned, copied or duplicated, or posted to "
