      "image": "slide_graphs/WEEK_5_PRODUCER_THEORY_s083.png",
      "vision_extract": "## Slide 83 Analysis: A Competitive Firm's Short Run Supply Curve\n\n### 1) Main Concept/Topic\nA competitive firm's short-run supply curve and the decision rules for production vs. shutdown based on price and cost relationships.\n\n### 2) Equations Shown\n- **P = MC** (profit-maximizing condition when P > AVC)\n- **Q = 0** (shutdown condition when P < AVC)\n\n### 3) Graph Details\n**Axes:**\n- Vertical axis: Costs\n- Horizontal axis: Q (Quantity)\n\n**Curves:**\n- **MC (Marginal Cost):** Red upward-sloping curve\n- **ATC (Average Total Cost):** Blue U-shaped curve\n- **AVC (Average Variable Cost):** Blue U-shaped curve, positioned below ATC\n\n**Key Points/Intersections:**\n- MC curve intersects both ATC and AVC at their minimum points\n- The intersection of MC and AVC represents the shutdown point\n- A red vertical line indicates the supply curve portion (MC above AVC)\n\n### 4) Key Definitions/Emphasized Text\n- **Production Rule:** \"If P > AVC, then the firm produces Q where P = MC\"\n- **Shutdown Rule:** \"If P < AVC, then firm shuts down (produces Q = 0)\"\n- **Supply Curve Definition:** \"The firm's short run supply curve is the portion of its MC curve above AVC\" (emphasized in blue text)\n\nThe slide demonstrates that a competitive firm's short-run supply curve consists only of the marginal cost curve above the average variable cost curve, with shutdown occurring below the AVC threshold.",
      "text": "A Competitive Firm\u2019s Short Run Supply Curve\nCosts\nMC\nIf P > AVC, then the\nfirm produces Q\nATC\nwhere P = MC.\nAVC\nIf P < AVC, then firm\nshuts down\n(produces Q = 0). Q\nThe firm\u2019s short run supply curve is the portion of its\nMC curve above AVC.\nMankiw, Principles of Economics, 10th Edition. \u00a9 2024 Cenga"
    },
