      "image": "slide_graphs/WEEK_5_PRODUCER_THEORY_s094.png",
      "vision_extract": "## Slide 94 Analysis: The Competitive Firm's Long Run Supply Curve\n\n### 1) Main Concept/Topic\nThe Competitive Firm's Long Run Supply Curve - specifically how a firm determines its long-run production decisions based on the relationship between price and average total cost.\n\n### 2) Equations Shown\nNo explicit equations are displayed on this slide.\n\n### 3) Graph Details\n**Axes:**\n- Vertical axis: Costs\n- Horizontal axis: Q (Quantity)\n\n**Curves:**\n- MC (Marginal Cost): Red upward-sloping curve\n- ATC (Average Total Cost): Blue U-shaped curve\n\n**Key Points/Intersections:**\n- MC and ATC curves intersect at the minimum point of the ATC curve\n- A horizontal pink/red line indicates a price level that intersects both curves\n\n**Labels:**\n- Both curves are clearly labeled as \"MC\" and \"ATC\"\n- Axes labeled as \"Costs\" and \"Q\"\n\n### 4) Key Definitions or Emphasized Text\n\n**Decision Rules:**\n- \"If P > ATC, then the firm produces Q on the MC curve\"\n- \"If P < ATC, then firm exits in the long run\"\n\n**Key Definition (emphasized in blue):**\n\"The firm's LR supply curve is the portion of its MC curve above ATC\"\n\n**Important Note:** This slide establishes that in the long run, firms will only remain in the market if they can cover their average total costs, making the relevant portion of the supply curve only the MC curve above the ATC curve.",
      "text": "The Competitive Firm\u2019s Long Run Supply Curve\nCosts\nMC\nIf P > ATC, then the\nfirm produces Q on\nATC\nthe MC curve.\nIf P < ATC, then firm\nexits in the long run.\nQ\nThe firm\u2019s LR supply curve is the portion of its MC\ncurve above ATC.\nMankiw, Principles of Economics, 10th Edition. \u00a9 2024 Cengage. All Right"
    },
