      "image": "slide_graphs/WEEK_5_PRODUCER_THEORY_s100.png",
      "vision_extract": "## Slide Analysis: Market Supply Assumptions\n\n### 1) Main Concept/Topic\nMarket Supply: Assumptions - foundational assumptions for deriving market supply curves in producer theory\n\n### 2) Equations Shown\nNone\n\n### 3) Graph Details\nNo graphs present\n\n### 4) Key Definitions or Emphasized Text\n\n**Three Core Assumptions:**\n\n1. **Identical Cost Curves**: All existing firms and potential entrants have identical cost curves\n\n2. **Cost Independence**: Each firm's costs do not change as other firms enter or exit the market\n\n3. **Number of Firms in Market**:\n   - **Fixed in the short run** (due to fixed costs)\n   - **Variable in the long run** (due to free entry and exit)\n\n**Key Terms Emphasized:**\n- \"identical cost curves\"\n- \"do not change\"\n- \"Fixed in the short run\"\n- \"Variable in the long run\"\n- \"due to fixed costs\"\n- \"due to free entry and exit\"\n\nThese assumptions establish the theoretical foundation for understanding how individual firm supply curves aggregate to form market supply, with important distinctions between short-run and long-run market dynamics.",
