      "image": "slide_graphs/WEEK_5_PRODUCER_THEORY_s101.png",
      "vision_extract": "## Economics Lecture Slide Analysis\n\n### 1) Main Concept/Topic\nShort Run Market Supply Curve - how market supply is derived from individual firm supply decisions in the short run\n\n### 2) Equations Shown\n- **P \u2265 AVC** (price must be greater than or equal to average variable cost)\n- **MR = MC** (marginal revenue equals marginal cost for profit maximization)\n\n### 3) Graph Details\nNo graph present on this slide\n\n### 4) Key Definitions or Emphasized Text\n\n**Key Condition (emphasized in red):**\n- \"As long as P \u2265 AVC\"\n\n**Profit Maximization Rule:**\n- Each firm will produce its profit-maximizing quantity where MR = MC\n\n**Market Supply Definition (emphasized in red):**\n- \"Market supply: The sum of all individual supplies\"\n\n**Reference to Previous Material (emphasized in red):**\n- \"Recall from Chapter 4:\" \n- At each price, the market quantity supplied is the sum of quantities supplied by all firms\n\n**Key Concepts:**\n- Individual firm behavior: firms produce where MR = MC when price covers average variable cost\n- Market aggregation: market supply curve is horizontal summation of all individual firm supply curves\n- Short-run constraint: firms continue operating as long as they can cover variable costs",
      "text": "Short Run Market Supply Curve\n\u2022 As long as P \u2265 AVC\n\u2013 Each firm will produce its profit-maximizing quantity,\nwhere MR = MC.\n\u2022 Recall from Chapter 4:\n\u2013 At each price, the market quantity supplied is the sum of\nquantities supplied by all firms\n\u2022 Market supply:\n\u2013 The sum of all individual supplies\n101\nM"
