      "image": "slide_graphs/WEEK_5_PRODUCER_THEORY_s102.png",
      "vision_extract": "## Slide 102 Analysis: The Short Run Market Supply Curve\n\n### 1) Main Concept/Topic\nThe derivation of the short run market supply curve from individual firm supply curves, demonstrating how market supply is constructed by horizontally summing individual firm supply curves.\n\n### 2) Equations Shown\n- **Market Qs = 1,000 \u00d7 (one firm's Qs)**\n\n### 3) Graph Details\n\n**Left Graph - One Firm:**\n- **Axes:** Price (P) on vertical axis, Quantity (Q firm) on horizontal axis\n- **Curves:** \n  - MC (Marginal Cost) curve - blue, upward sloping\n  - AVC (Average Variable Cost) curve - blue, U-shaped\n- **Key Points:** \n  - Three price levels: P\u2081 (green), P\u2082 (red), P\u2083 (red)\n  - Corresponding quantities: 10, 20, 30 units\n- **Note:** Supply curve follows MC curve above AVC minimum\n\n**Right Graph - Market:**\n- **Axes:** Price (P) on vertical axis, Quantity (Q market) on horizontal axis\n- **Curves:** S (Market Supply) curve - blue, upward sloping\n- **Key Points:**\n  - Same price levels: P\u2081, P\u2082, P\u2083\n  - Corresponding market quantities: 10,000, 20,000, 30,000 units\n- **Intersections:** Horizontal price lines intersect supply curve at specified quantities\n\n### 4) Key Definitions/Emphasized Text\n- **Assumption:** \"1,000 identical firms\"\n- **Relationship:** \"At each P, market Qs = 1,000 \u00d7 (one firm's Qs)\"\n- **Scaling factor:** Individual firm quantities multiplied by 1,000 to obtain market quantities\n- The market supply curve maintains the same shape as individual firm supply but scaled horizontally by the number of firms",
      "text": "The Short Run Market Supply Curve\n\u2022 Assume: 1,000 identical firms\n\u2022 At each P, market Qs = 1,000 x (one firm\u2019s Qs)\nOne firm Market\nP P\nMC S\nP P\n3 3\nP P\n2 2\nAVC\nP P\n1 1\nQ Q\n10 20 30\n(firm) (market\n)\n10,000 20,000 30,000\nMankiw, Principles of Economics, 10th Edition. \u00a9 2024 Cengage. All Rights Reserve"
    },
