      "image": "slide_graphs/WEEK_6_PRODUCER_THEORY_II_s111.png",
      "vision_extract": "## Slide 111 Analysis: Long Run Equilibrium\n\n### 1) Main Concept/Topic\nLong Run Equilibrium in monopolistically competitive markets - explaining the adjustment process from short-run profits/losses to long-run zero economic profit equilibrium\n\n### 2) Equations Shown\nNone\n\n### 3) Graph Details\nNo graphs present on this slide\n\n### 4) Key Definitions and Emphasized Text\n\n**Main Topic (Header):** \"Long Run Equilibrium\"\n\n**Two Primary Scenarios:**\n\n**Scenario 1 - Short Run Profits:**\n- **Condition:** \"If monopolistically competitive firms are making profit in short run\"\n- **Market Entry Process:**\n  - New firms have incentive to enter the market\n  - Entry increases number of products\n  - Reduces demand faced by each existing firm\n  - Demand curve shifts left, causing prices to fall\n  - **End Result:** Each firm's profit declines to zero\n\n**Scenario 2 - Short Run Losses:**\n- **Condition:** \"If losses in the short run\"\n- **Market Exit Process:**\n  - Some firms exit the market\n  - **End Result:** Remaining firms enjoy higher demand and prices\n\n**Key Economic Principle:** The slide demonstrates the self-correcting mechanism in monopolistic competition where entry/exit drives economic profits to zero in the long run, regardless of whether firms start with profits or losses in the short run.",
      "text": "Long Run Equilibrium\n\u2022 If monopolistically competitive firms are\nmaking profit in short run\n\u2013 New firms: incentive to enter the market\n\u2022 Increase number of products\n\u2013 Reduces demand faced by each firm\n\u2022 Demand curve shifts left; prices fall\n\u2013 Each firm\u2019s profit declines to zero\n\u2022 If losses in the sh"
    },
