      "vision_extract": "## Slide 126 Analysis: Understanding Best Responses\n\n### 1) Main Concept/Topic\n**Game Theory - Best Response Analysis in Strategic Decision Making**\n- Using payoff matrices to determine optimal player strategies\n- Analysis of conditional strategy selection based on opponent's known actions\n\n### 2) Equations Shown\nNo mathematical equations present on this slide.\n\n### 3) Graph Details\n**Payoff Matrix Structure:**\n- **Format:** 2\u00d72 strategic game matrix\n- **Players:** Anil (row player, blue label on left side) vs. Bala (column player, purple header)\n- **Strategies:** \n  - Anil's options: Rice, Cassava\n  - Bala's options: Rice, Cassava\n- **Payoff Values:**\n  - (Rice, Rice): Anil gets 3, Bala gets 1\n  - (Rice, Cassava): Anil gets 2, Bala gets 2  \n  - (Cassava, Rice): Anil gets 4, Bala gets 4\n  - (Cassava, Cassava): Anil gets 1, Bala gets 3\n- **Matrix Layout:** Anil's payoffs shown in upper portion of each cell, Bala's in lower portion\n\n### 4) Key Definitions or Emphasized Text\n- **\"Best response\"** (emphasized): The strategy that will give a player the highest payoff, given the strategies that the other players select\n- **Key example provided:** \"If Bala chooses Rice, Anil's best response is to choose Cassava\u2014that gives him 4, rather than 1\"\n- **Core principle:** Payoff matrix enables analysis of optimal player actions under complete information about opponent's strategy",
      "text": "Understanding best responses\nWe can use the payoff matrix to analyse what action a player would\nchoose if he knew what the other player will do.\nBest response: The strategy that\nwill give a player the highest payoff,\ngiven the strategies that the other\nplayers select.\nIf Bala chooses Rice, Anil\u2019s be"
