      "vision_extract": "## Slide 59 Analysis: Four Properties of Indifference Curves\n\n### 1. Main Concept/Topic\nProperty 2 of indifference curves: Higher indifference curves represent preferred consumption bundles compared to lower indifference curves\n\n### 2. Equations Shown\nNone\n\n### 3. Graph Details\n**Axes:**\n- X-axis: Quantity of Fish\n- Y-axis: Quantity of Mangos\n\n**Curves:**\n- Three downward-sloping indifference curves labeled I\u2080, I\u2081, and I\u2082\n- Curves are convex to the origin\n- Higher curves (I\u2082) are positioned further from the origin than lower curves (I\u2080)\n\n**Key Points:**\n- Point A: Located on indifference curve I\u2081\n- Point C: Located on indifference curve I\u2082\n- Point D: Located on indifference curve I\u2080\n\n**Intersections:**\n- No curve intersections shown (indifference curves do not cross)\n\n### 4. Key Definitions or Emphasized Text\n**Main Property (emphasized in red):**\n\"Higher indifference curves are preferred to lower ones.\"\n\n**Explanation:**\n- Hurley prefers every bundle on I\u2082 (like point C) to every bundle on I\u2081 (like point A)\n- He prefers every bundle on I\u2081 (like point A) to every bundle on I\u2080 (like point D)\n- This demonstrates the transitivity of preferences across indifference curve levels\n\n**Additional Context:**\n- Title indicates this is part of \"A few of Hurley's indifference curves\"\n- This is the second of four fundamental properties of indifference curves in consumer choice theory",
