      "vision_extract": "## Slide Analysis: Economics Lecture - Theory of Consumer Choice\n\n### 1. Main Concept/Topic\n**Four Properties of Indifference Curves - Property 3: Indifference curves cannot cross**\n\n### 2. Equations Shown\nNo equations present on this slide.\n\n### 3. Graph Details\n- **Axes**: \n  - X-axis: Quantity of Fish\n  - Y-axis: Quantity of Mangos\n- **Curves**: Two indifference curves labeled I\u2081 (orange/lower curve) and I\u2084 (blue/upper curve)\n- **Key Points**: \n  - Point A: Located on both curves at their intersection\n  - Point B: Located on the upper curve (I\u2084) \n  - Point C: Located on the lower curve (I\u2081)\n- **Intersections**: The curves cross at point A (demonstrating the theoretical impossibility being explained)\n\n### 4. Key Definitions/Emphasized Text\n- **Main Property (in red)**: \"Indifference curves cannot cross\"\n- **Logical contradiction explanation**:\n  - Hurley should prefer B to C (since B has more of both goods)\n  - Yet Hurley is indifferent between B and C\n  - Hurley likes C as much as A (both on I\u2084)\n  - Hurley likes A as much as B (both on I\u2081)\n- **Reference to consumer**: \"Hurley\" used as the example consumer\n- **Curve notation**: I\u2081 and I\u2084 represent different indifference curves with different utility levels\n\nThe slide demonstrates why crossing indifference curves violate the transitivity assumption in consumer theory.",
      "text": "Four Properties of Indifference Curves\nIndifference curves cannot cross.\n3.\nSuppose they did. Hurley\u2019s\nQuantity\nof Mangos indifference curves\nHurley should prefer\nB to C, since B has\nmore of both goods.\nYet, Hurley is indifferent\nB\nbetween B and C:\nC\nHe likes C as much as A A\nI\n(both are on I ). I\n4"
