      "vision_extract": "## 1) Main Concept/Topic\n**Fourth Property of Indifference Curves**: Indifference curves are bowed inward (convex to the origin)\n\n## 2) Equations Shown\nNone present\n\n## 3) Graph Details\n**Axes:**\n- X-axis: Quantity of Fish\n- Y-axis: Quantity of Mangos\n\n**Curves:**\n- Single indifference curve labeled \"I\u2081\"\n- Curve is bowed inward/convex to the origin\n\n**Key Points:**\n- Point A: Located at approximately (1, 6) - representing 1 fish and 6 mangos\n- Point B: Located at approximately (2, 1) - representing 2 fish and 1 mango\n\n**Labels:**\n- Consumer name: \"Hurley\"\n- Indifference curve labeled as \"I\u2081\"\n\n## 4) Key Definitions or Emphasized Text\n**Main Property (highlighted in red):**\n\"4. Indifference curves are bowed inward.\"\n\n**Economic Interpretation:**\n\"Hurley is willing to give up more mangos for a fish if he has few fish (A) than if he has many (B).\"\n\n**Economic Concept Illustrated:**\nThis demonstrates the principle of diminishing marginal rate of substitution - as a consumer has less of one good, they are willing to give up more of the other good to obtain an additional unit of the scarce good.",
      "text": "Four Properties of Indifference Curves\nIndifference curves are bowed inward.\n4.\nQuantity\nof Mangos\nHurley is willing to\ngive up more\nA\nmangos for a fish if\nhe has few fish (A)\n6\nthan if he has many\n1\n(B).\nB\n2\n1 I\n1\nQuantity\nof Fish\n\u00a9 2018 Cengage Learning\u00ae. May not be scanned, copied or duplicated, "
