      "image": "slide_graphs/WEEK_8_THEORY_OF_CONSUMER_CHOICE_s062.png",
      "vision_extract": "## Slide 62 Analysis: The Marginal Rate of Substitution\n\n### 1) Main Concept/Topic\n**The Marginal Rate of Substitution (MRS)** - specifically explaining how MRS represents the rate at which a consumer is willing to trade one good for another, and demonstrating the diminishing MRS along an indifference curve.\n\n### 2) Equations Shown\n- **MRS = slope of indifference curve (absolute value)**\n- Specific numerical examples:\n  - **MRS = 6** (at point A)\n  - **MRS = 2** (at point B)\n\n### 3) Graph Details\n**Axes:**\n- X-axis: Quantity of Fish\n- Y-axis: Quantity of Mangos\n\n**Curve:**\n- Downward-sloping indifference curve labeled **I\u2081**\n\n**Key Points:**\n- **Point A:** Higher on the curve, MRS = 6\n- **Point B:** Lower on the curve, MRS = 2\n\n**Intersections/Measurements:**\n- Vertical distance of 1 unit and horizontal distance measurements shown to illustrate slope calculations at both points\n\n### 4) Key Definitions or Emphasized Text\n**Primary Definition (in red text):**\n- **Marginal rate of substitution (MRS):** the rate at which a consumer is willing to trade one good for another.\n\n**Supporting Explanations:**\n- \"Hurley's MRS is the amount of mangos he would substitute for another fish\"\n- **Key Property:** \"MRS falls as you move down along an indifference curve\"\n- MRS equals the absolute value of the slope of the indifference curve (highlighted in pink box)",
      "text": "The Marginal Rate of Substitution\nMarginal rate of\nMRS = slope of\nsubstitution (MRS):\nindifference curve\nQuantity\nthe rate at which a\nof Mangos\n(absolute value)\nconsumer is willing to\ntrade one good for another.\nA\nMRS = 6\nHurley\u2019s MRS is the\namount of mangos he\n1\nwould substitute for another\nB\nMRS ="
