      "image": "slide_graphs/WEEK_8_THEORY_OF_CONSUMER_CHOICE_s065.png",
      "vision_extract": "## Slide 65 Analysis - Perfect Substitutes\n\n### 1) Main Concept/Topic\nPerfect Substitutes in consumer choice theory - an extreme case of consumer preferences where goods can be substituted at a constant rate.\n\n### 2) Equations Shown\nNo explicit equations are shown on this slide.\n\n### 3) Graph Details\n**Axes:**\n- X-axis: Dimes (horizontal axis, scale 0-3)\n- Y-axis: Nickels (vertical axis, scale 0-6)\n\n**Curves:**\n- Three parallel straight-line indifference curves labeled I\u2081, I\u2082, I\u2083\n- All curves have identical negative slopes\n- Curves are positioned further from origin indicating higher utility levels (I\u2081 < I\u2082 < I\u2083)\n\n**Key Points:**\n- Points marked on each indifference curve at intersections with grid\n- I\u2081 intersects axes at approximately (1,0) and (0,2)\n- I\u2082 intersects axes at approximately (2,0) and (0,4)\n- I\u2083 intersects axes at approximately (3,0) and (0,6)\n\n**Labels:**\n- Indifference curves labeled as I\u2081, I\u2082, I\u2083\n\n### 4) Key Definitions or Emphasized Text\n- **Perfect substitutes** (highlighted in red): two goods with straight-line indifference curves, constant MRS\n- **Example**: nickels and dimes\n- **Key behavioral assumption**: \"Consumer is always willing to trade two nickels for one dime\"\n- **Constant MRS**: The marginal rate of substitution remains constant along each indifference curve (slope = -2, indicating 2 nickels = 1 dime)",
      "text": "One Extreme Case: Perfect Substitutes\nPerfect substitutes: two goods with\nstraight-line indifference curves,\nconstant MRS\nExample: nickels and dimes\nConsumer is always willing to\ntrade two nickels for one dime.\n\u00a9 2018 Cengage Learning\u00ae. May not be scanned, copied or duplicated, or posted to a public"
