      "image": "slide_graphs/WEEK_8_THEORY_OF_CONSUMER_CHOICE_s066.png",
      "vision_extract": "## Slide 66 Analysis: Perfect Complements\n\n### 1) Main Concept/Topic\nPerfect complements as an extreme case in consumer choice theory, demonstrating goods that must be consumed in fixed proportions.\n\n### 2) Equations Shown\nNo equations are displayed on this slide.\n\n### 3) Graph Details\n**Axes:**\n- X-axis: Right Shoes\n- Y-axis: Left Shoes\n\n**Curves:**\n- Two right-angle (L-shaped) indifference curves labeled I\u2081 and I\u2082\n- I\u2081 (lower curve): corner at point (5, 5)\n- I\u2082 (higher curve): corner at point (7, 7)\n\n**Key Points:**\n- Green point at (5, 5) on curve I\u2081\n- Red point at (5, 7) on curve I\u2082\n- Black point at (7, 5) also on curve I\u2081\n\n**Labels:**\n- Dotted lines showing coordinates at x=5, x=7, y=5, y=7\n\n### 4) Key Definitions and Emphasized Text\n**Main Definition (in red):**\n\"Perfect complements: two goods with right-angle indifference curves\"\n\n**Example provided:**\nLeft shoes and right shoes as the practical illustration\n\n**Key relationship highlighted:**\n\"{7 left shoes, 5 right shoes} is just as good as {5 left shoes, 5 right shoes}\"\n\nThis demonstrates that excess of one good provides no additional utility when the other good is the limiting factor in the fixed-proportion relationship.",
      "text": "Another Extreme Case: Perfect Complements\nPerfect complements: two goods\nwith right-angle indifference curves\nExample: Left shoes, right shoes\n{7 left shoes, 5 right shoes}\nis just as good as\n{5 left shoes, 5 right shoes}\n\u00a9 2018 Cengage Learning\u00ae. May not be scanned, copied or duplicated, or posted "
    },
