      "vision_extract": "## Slide 88 Analysis - Economics Lecture\n\n### 1) Main Concept/Topic\n**Inferior vs. Normal Goods** - Examining how income changes affect demand for different types of goods in consumer choice theory\n\n### 2) Equations Shown\nNone present\n\n### 3) Graph Details\nNo graph is displayed on this slide. However, the slide instructs students to create a diagram showing the effects of income increase on Hurley's optimal bundle of fish and mangos.\n\n### 4) Key Definitions and Emphasized Text\n\n**Key Economic Principle (emphasized):**\n- An increase in income increases the quantity demanded of **normal goods** and reduces the quantity demanded of **inferior goods**\n\n**Definitions by Example:**\n- **Normal good**: Fish (demand increases with income)\n- **Inferior good**: Mangos (demand decreases with income)\n\n**Assignment/Exercise:**\n- Students are instructed to \"Use a diagram to show the effects of an increase in income on Hurley's optimal bundle of fish and mangos\"\n\n**Context:** This is Active Learning Exercise 3, focusing on the practical application of income effects on consumer choice between normal and inferior goods.",
