      "vision_extract": "## Slide 93 Analysis: Deriving Hurley's Demand Curve for Fish\n\n### 1) Main Concept/Topic\n- **Deriving an individual demand curve from consumer choice theory**\n- Demonstration of how optimal consumption points at different prices create a demand curve\n\n### 2) Equations Shown\n- No explicit equations displayed\n- **Implicit price-quantity relationships:**\n  - Point A: PF = $4, QF = 150 fish\n  - Point B: PF = $2, QF = 350 fish\n\n### 3) Graph Details\n\n**Left Graph (Consumer Choice/Indifference Curve Analysis):**\n- **X-axis:** Quantity of Fish\n- **Y-axis:** Quantity of Mangos\n- **Key elements:**\n  - Two budget constraint lines (blue and red)\n  - Two indifference curves (blue and red)\n  - Point A: intersection at 150 fish quantity\n  - Point B: intersection at 350 fish quantity\n  - Red arrows indicating the horizontal projection of optimal points\n\n**Right Graph (Demand Curve):**\n- **X-axis:** Quantity of Fish\n- **Y-axis:** Price of Fish\n- **Key elements:**\n  - Green downward-sloping demand curve labeled \"DFish\"\n  - Point A: (150 fish, $4) marked with black dot\n  - Point B: (350 fish, $2) marked with black dot\n  - Horizontal dashed lines at $4 and $2 price levels\n  - Vertical dashed lines at 150 and 350 quantity levels\n  - Red arrows showing the transfer of points from left graph\n\n### 4) Key Definitions or Emphasized Text\n- **Point A:** \"When PF = $4, Hurley demands 150 fish\"\n- **Point B:** \"When PF = $2, Hurley demands 350 fish\"\n- **Main concept:** The slide demonstrates the derivation process of an individual demand curve by connecting optimal consumption points at different price levels",
      "text": "Deriving Hurley\u2019s Demand Curve for Fish\nA: When P = $4, Hurley demands 150 fish.\nF\nB: When P = $2, Hurley demands 350 fish.\nF\nQuantity Price of\nof Mangos Fish\nA\n$4\nA\nB\nB\n$2\nD\nFish\n150 350 Quantity 150 350 Quantity\nof Fish of Fish\n\u00a9 2018 Cengage Learning\u00ae. May not be scanned, copied or duplicated, or"
