      "vision_extract": "## SLIDE 70 ANALYSIS: Nominal and Real GDP in the U.S., 1965-2022\n\n### 1) MAIN CONCEPT/TOPIC\nComparison of U.S. Nominal GDP versus Real GDP trends over a 57-year period (1965-2022), demonstrating the difference between inflation-adjusted and non-inflation-adjusted economic output measures.\n\n### 2) EQUATIONS SHOWN\nNone present.\n\n### 3) GRAPH DETAILS\n\n**Axes:**\n- X-axis: Time period from 1970 to 2020 (in 10-year intervals)\n- Y-axis: Billions of Dollars, Billions of Chained 2012 Dollars (scale from 0 to 28,000)\n\n**Curves:**\n- Blue line: Gross Domestic Product (Nominal GDP)\n- Red line: Real Gross Domestic Product (Real GDP)\n\n**Key Points:**\n- Both lines start close together around 1965 (approximately 4,000-5,000 billion)\n- Lines diverge significantly over time, with nominal GDP rising more steeply\n- By 2022: Nominal GDP reaches approximately 25,000 billion; Real GDP reaches approximately 20,000 billion\n- Gray shaded vertical bars indicate recession periods throughout the timeline\n\n**Intersections:**\n- Lines cross around 2012, which aligns with the base year notation for Real GDP\n\n### 4) KEY DEFINITIONS OR EMPHASIZED TEXT\n- **Real GDP (base year 2012)**: Explicitly noted on the graph, indicating inflation-adjusted GDP using 2012 as the reference year\n- **Source attribution**: U.S. Bureau of Economic Analysis\n- **Data source**: FRED (Federal Reserve Economic Data)",
