      "vision_extract": "## Slide 103 Analysis: Active Learning 4 - Comparing Tuition Increases\n\n### 1) Main Concept/Topic\n**Real vs. nominal tuition cost analysis** - Using Consumer Price Index (CPI) to adjust historical tuition data for inflation and calculate real percentage increases across different types of U.S. higher education institutions from 1990 to 2022.\n\n### 2) Equations Shown\nNo explicit equations are displayed, but the exercise implicitly requires:\n- Real value calculation: Real 1990 tuition = (Nominal 1990 tuition \u00d7 2022 CPI) \u00f7 1990 CPI\n- Percentage change formula: ((2022 value - Real 1990 value) \u00f7 Real 1990 value) \u00d7 100\n\n### 3) Graph Details\n**No graphs present** - Only tabular data is shown.\n\n### 4) Key Definitions and Data Structure\n\n**Data Table: \"Tuition and Fees at U.S. Colleges and Universities\"**\n\n| Institution Type | 1990 | 2022 |\n|-----------------|------|------|\n| Private non-profit 4-year | $9,340 | $38,070 |\n| Public 4-year (in state) | $1,908 | $10,740 |\n| Public 2-year | $906 | $3,800 |\n| CPI | 130.7 | 292.3 |\n\n**Key Learning Questions:**\n- **Part A:** Convert 1990 tuition figures to 2022 dollars using CPI adjustment, then calculate real percentage increases for all three institution types\n- **Part B:** Identify which institution type experienced the largest real tuition cost increase\n\n**Emphasized Elements:**\n- CPI data provided for inflation adjustment calculations\n- Three distinct categories of higher education institutions for comparison\n- Focus on **real** (inflation-adjusted) rather than nominal increases",
